00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.370
I know to many of you,
you've seen these slides.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:43.050
So I won't spend too
much time on them.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.860
But it's always good to
start with a quick foundation

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.320
of where we are and
how this matches

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.420
with our current offering set.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.540
So Apigee is a full lifecycle
API management product.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.400
And obviously, what does
full life cycle mean?

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.420
It goes back to the beginning
of, what is an API lifecycle?

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.730
Any good API should
start with API design.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.870
And if you had asked me a
couple of years ago API design,

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.840
I would have always said
open API specification--

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.350
that's all [INAUDIBLE] was
known a few years ago--

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.090
would have been a
great place to start.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:21.930
But as we're seeing today, APIs
come in other shapes and sizes.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:26.140
We're seeing a lot of GraphQL
being adopted by enterprises.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.750
We are also seeing GRPC
as a popular form factor.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.990
So API design now is more
than the Open API spec.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:36.290
But fundamentally, the fact
that you start with a design

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:37.890
hasn't changed.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.580
And then the natural
evolution of that

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.250
API is when you go into
developing that API,

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:45.890
securing that API,
and deploying it.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.440
And it is really
deploy that we are

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.210
going to talk more about today.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:51.830
Where do you deploy it?

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.530
What has changed
in the form factor

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.610
of where do you deploy is what
we are going to talk today.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.820
The rest of the pieces
of publish, monitor,

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.440
analyze, and monetize
really hasn't changed.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.390
But we'll briefly go over them
as we talk about deploying

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.800
APIs in multiple platforms.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.130
So this is the
slide that we have

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:17.810
that talks about how Apigee
fits into a typical enterprise.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:20.960
Your enterprise has many
types of applications.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.090
You're probably building
your applications

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:24.470
with micro services.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.230
You still carry a lot
of legacy applications,

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.180
and they will not
necessarily be moved.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:33.230
And you're also consuming
APIs built by other vendors--

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:37.730
from Salesforce, from Workday,
from other SaaS providers.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:40.010
And at the end of
it, API management

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:42.782
is super critical for
the consumption layer,

00:03:42.782 --> 00:03:44.240
because at the end
of the day, what

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:47.660
you want to do is take all
these assets, these business

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.400
assets that you
have, and make them

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.630
available to your consumers.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.640
So if I had to
succinctly put, what

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.610
does API management provide?

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:59.420
It manages the relationship
between providers

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:04.250
and consumers-- providers of
APIs and consumers of APIs.

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:08.270
So from that perspective,
we've got three or four

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.160
fundamental building blocks.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.630
We've got the runtime.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:15.530
We've got monitoring, analytics,
and of course, the developer

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.149
ecosystem.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:21.209
So how do these impact
from a hybrid perspective?

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:23.420
Or what specifically
does it change

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:24.980
from a hybrid perspective?

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.710
Let's focus first
on the runtime.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.490
From the runtime perspective,
we had three types of gateways

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:33.200
available today.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.510
In the Cloud, as
well as on premises,

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.480
we had the enterprise gateway.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.120
For hybrid, we had
microgateway and Istio

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.250
as the three types
of gateways that we

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.680
had available to customers.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.380
From a mediation
perspective, this

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.460
is an API provider trying
to connect that API

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:54.710
to different sets of system.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.970
The use cases that we saw
were security, transformation,

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:04.730
orchestration, abuse prevention,
and of course, extensions.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:07.400
Some of these features
we introduced recently.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.380
Take, for example, extensions.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.860
We announced extensions
in the second quarter

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.430
of last year, which provides
developers with the ability

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:18.850
to connect to Cloud Services,
like data loss prevention,

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:22.150
to mask or obfuscate
the API content,

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:23.980
to connect to,
say, Google pub/sub

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:26.530
as an integration to
your messaging platform.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:28.900
And now we have
started adding services

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:34.030
that are outside of GCP, like
with AWS and also Salesforce.

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:36.910
So extensions and
the runtime assets

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:40.360
are really providing those
tools to the developer

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.750
and improving the API
provider's productivity

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.090
by giving them tools
to build and connect

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.550
to different sets of APIs.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:52.690
In the same vein, security
is also an important aspect

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.520
of exposing APIs.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.100
You probably want to
do security mediation

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.140
because not all of
your applications

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:00.940
talk the same security language.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.070
And it's important to
mediate between them.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:05.680
So from a provider's
perspective,

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:07.600
as you're building
APIs, you also

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.090
want to be able to
monitor and analyze them.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:14.230
And these are typically
different personas

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:15.580
that look at this.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:17.470
The persona of a
production operator

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:20.200
is going to look at your
fleet-wide set of APIs

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.090
and look at, how are
all my APIs doing?

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.250
Are they throwing errors?

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.100
Are they measuring or
keeping up to the latency?

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.270
Is it not too high,
and things like that.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:31.090
But as an API
provider, I don't care

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.250
about the fleet-wide
aspects of my API program.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:37.360
I really care about APIs
that I have developed.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:38.945
And for me, things
that are important

00:06:38.945 --> 00:06:42.610
is, which is a consumer
that's consuming my API?

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:45.670
What type of client devices
are they connecting from?

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:50.140
If I am upgrading my API,
who gets impacted, and so on.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:53.710
So different sets of personas,
but these are also critical

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:55.750
from an API
management perspective

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:59.180
that you have all this
data available to them.

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:02.050
And last-- and perhaps most
importantly-- all of this

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:05.830
is done to make the
consumers highly productive.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:09.880
You want consumers to find a
single catalog of all the APIs

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.740
that are eligible to access.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:14.540
And once they can access them,
that they can request access

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:15.040
to it.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.470
And you, as a
provider, can manage

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:22.300
who or which of your consumers
can get access to these APIs,

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.950
how much of it can
they get, and so on.

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:27.010
So it really comes down to this.

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:30.340
The catalog of the portal
becomes that key place

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:33.130
where you manage that
relationship of consumers

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:36.340
coming in, hopefully some
service that they can auto

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:39.580
register, come in, see that
set of APIs that they have.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.130
And you as a provider
are deciding how much

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:44.290
and whom you want
to grant access to.

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:46.030
And this follows
the fundamentals

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.620
of an API management platform.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:51.550
So with this what
really has changed?

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:54.550
What we have seen
is that enterprises

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:58.510
are in a flux of moving
workloads from their data

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:00.130
centers to clouds.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:02.710
And a lot of times,
it is lift and shift.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:06.770
And in some cases, it's also
life, modernize, and shift.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:10.030
So what does that mean
for API management?

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:13.210
What it means for us is
that in our enterprises,

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:17.950
we see that APIs are going
to be in multiple spaces.

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:20.800
Some APIs are going
to be on Cloud.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.800
And usually enterprises are
picking more than one cloud

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:26.500
provider because they don't want
to put all their eggs in one

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:27.357
basket.

00:08:27.357 --> 00:08:29.440
And there are going to be
some APIs that are never

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:30.580
going to leave on premises.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.409
And on premises, our
data [INAUDIBLE] data

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:34.990
center footprint is
going to be a reality

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:36.770
for a lot of these enterprises.

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:39.610
And that's not going
to change, either.

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:42.880
So Apigee's current
offering, which is primarily

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:45.850
in two-fold, which is a
completely SaaS offering

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:49.210
or a completely on premises
offering with what customers

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:51.640
had the ability to pick from.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:54.010
Now, the completely SaaS
offering, of course,

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:55.960
worked when you had
your workloads already

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:57.940
moved to the Cloud,
or if they already

00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:59.320
remained in the data center.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:02.950
And the Cloud platform
could route requests

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.230
either to one or
more Cloud providers

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:07.030
or to your data center.

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:08.950
For the enterprises
that were kind

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:11.440
of apprehensive about shifting
workloads to the Cloud,

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:14.470
they had the fully on
premises version of Apigee.

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:17.300
And then they could run
it in their data centers.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:18.470
So what really changed?

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:20.590
What was missing?

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:23.020
One concern that
customers have expressed

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:26.650
with using a fully SaaS
offering was latency.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:30.040
Imagine this use case where
your consumers and providers

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.720
are in the same
Cloud or data center.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.380
You really don't want to
make a round trip all the way

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:37.090
to the Cloud to come back in.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:40.930
What you want is to have a
gateway much closer to you.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:44.890
From a security perspective,
some enterprises have standards

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:49.240
where certain API traffic cannot
leave their private networks.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.150
And again, a Cloud
offering in that case

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.380
doesn't make sense
because you have your API

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:56.740
call leave your network.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.260
So in both of these cases,
we would have told customers

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.020
that they could use Apigee's
on premises offering

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:04.030
and keep that traffic private.

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:08.050
The problem with using a fully
on premises version of Apigee

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:10.990
is that now there's an
overhead that customers

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:15.722
have to maintain-- the UI,
the RBAC, or the SSO for this.

00:10:15.722 --> 00:10:17.680
They have to maintain
all of the analytics that

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.638
they're [? making ?] the
[? photo. ?] They have

00:10:19.638 --> 00:10:21.670
to maintain really
the entire stack.

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:23.530
And what really they
were looking for

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:26.980
was the ability to keep the
gateways as close to the APIs

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:29.140
as possible and not
necessarily take

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:31.490
on the entire infrastructure.

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:33.040
So those are sort
of the beginnings

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:35.000
of why this was needed.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:38.890
So Apigee had defined
API management.

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:40.720
And it started this in 2015.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.580
And we'll show you
some examples of how

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:47.690
we did that of defining hybrid
API management as being this--

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:51.520
that there is an Apigee managed
control plane that is hosted by

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:53.890
and is maintained by
Apigee in the Cloud.

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.470
And then there's a
runtime plane that

00:10:56.470 --> 00:11:00.640
is hosted, managed, maintained
by customers on other Cloud

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.120
providers or in their
own data centers.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:07.600
And the runtime plane
contacted the control plane

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.960
to receive instructions
so it can operate it.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:15.130
What this decoupled architecture
gave customers is the ability

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:19.300
to deploy just the gateways as
close to workloads as possible.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:22.300
That way, latency was
no longer an issue.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:24.850
API traffic never
left your network

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:26.170
and it could stay local.

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:28.360
And yet, you didn't
have the overhead

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:31.330
of taking the entire
infrastructure

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:33.740
and making it available.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.250
So what really is part
of this management plane?

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:39.280
So the management plane
typically includes the UI.

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:44.060
It includes the RBAC for who can
do what even in the platform.

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:46.870
The UI can optionally
include the portal.

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:49.510
And I say optionally
because some customers

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:52.450
like to have custom portals
for various reasons.

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:55.150
And they should, of course,
continue to have that ability

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:56.620
to have custom portals.

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:58.240
And of course, the analytics--

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.540
I want to have these
gateways asynchronously send

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:04.600
analytics information
back to the control plane

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.990
where we can aggregate
this and create dashboards.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:10.450
So that is hybrid
API management,

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:13.270
to have a decoupled management
and a runtime plane.

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:16.300
You'll notice that I
interchangeably use the word

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:17.770
management and control plane.

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:18.280
Yes.

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:19.850
There are some
subtle differences.

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:21.880
But for the purposes
of this conversation,

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:24.490
I will interchangeably use
those words, a management plane

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:26.150
or a control plane.

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:27.730
And then you have
the runtime plane

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:30.460
that can be distributed
running where ever, which

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:34.030
is receiving instructions
from this management place.

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:39.520
So Apigee had two offerings--
or has two offerings--

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:41.470
for hybrid.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:45.310
In 2015, we launched
microgateway.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.680
And microgateway
was specifically

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.750
addressing the need for an
API gateway for microservices.

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:55.660
When enterprises started
to build microservices--

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:59.750
and these are the really early
days of microservices in 2015--

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:02.500
they were looking for a very
lightweight gateway that

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:06.970
could do basic API protection
as close to the microservice

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:10.570
as possible, things like
API key verification,

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:14.470
OAuth verification,
[INAUDIBLE] were all

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:17.860
critically needed for the
API as close as possible.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:19.510
A more centralized
gateway would have

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:21.440
mean that every one
of your microservices

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:23.440
would have to go through
the centralized gateway

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.830
and come back, incurring
a lot of latency

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:27.820
to your microservices.

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:31.300
So they really wanted a
lightweight gateway there.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:35.350
And micro gateway continues to
be adopted by our enterprises.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:38.590
And last year we did
a presentation with HP

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:42.160
where we talked about how HP
modernized their microservices

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:43.870
using microgateway.

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:47.737
So microgateway continues to
fill that segment of use cases

00:13:47.737 --> 00:13:50.320
where it makes sense where you
have a-- where you are building

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:53.410
microservices and you need
a gateway as close to it

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:54.890
as possible.

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:57.880
Last year, Google
announced Istio.

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.920
Istio is as an open
source platform

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:04.000
that Google announced with other
partners like Red Hat, IBM,

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.550
and now there are many more
people like F5 networks,

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:07.390
and so on.

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:12.100
And Istio provides a service
mesh management infrastructure,

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:14.660
or a service mesh
infrastructure.

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:17.500
So Istio is meant to
manage your microservices.

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:20.680
It provides features
that a service operator

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.290
or a microservice
operator would care for,

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:25.850
things that you want built
into your infrastructure

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:28.780
so you don't want
every service developer

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:32.050
to think about, things
like MTLS, routing,

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.720
load balancing,
fundamental aspects

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:38.050
that not every service
developer should think about.

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:41.710
So as Istio service mesh
started becoming popular

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:44.770
and we're seeing
enterprises adopt Istio,

00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:47.620
Istio provides a very
interesting capability.

00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:51.640
It adds the notion of a
sidecar to every service

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:52.450
that you build.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:55.360
And that's what you
see here as the proxy.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:58.700
Now, when you already have a
sidecar associated with it,

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:01.720
Apigee said, well, this is
a very interesting use case

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:03.820
to do API management, as well.

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:05.290
The sidecar already exists.

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:09.020
And the sidecar is capable
of enforcing policies.

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:12.860
So what we did in 2018 was
to extend the capabilities

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:16.490
of Istio, make it knowledgeable
about Apigee policies,

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:19.620
such as same API key
verification, OAuth,

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:24.080
[INAUDIBLE],, and all that, and
have the site sidecar proxy

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:26.460
implement it or enforce it.

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:29.900
So in 2018, we launched
another hybrid offering

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:32.930
for enterprises that
have adopted Istio

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:34.610
to manage their microservices.

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:38.720
They now get an Istio that
is capable of Apigee API

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:40.790
management policies.

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:42.380
So what then changed?

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:45.350
We see that there's an
evolving set of use cases,

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:49.730
that customers, while
microgateway and Istio

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:52.400
certainly fit a certain
set of use cases,

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:56.180
customers really wanted the
capabilities of the enterprise

00:15:56.180 --> 00:16:00.770
gateway available to them where
their APIs are without having

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:05.480
to go full fledged [? OPDK. ?]
So I've got my APIs in GCP.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:07.340
I've got my APIs in Azure.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:09.920
And I've got some APIs
in my data center.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:12.230
And I want to manage
them the same way

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:14.540
I would do it in the Cloud.

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:17.760
So there are two fundamental
requirements that came up.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:21.290
The first is that I want to
maintain my API proxy bundle

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:22.110
format.

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:24.200
So for those of you who
have been using Apigee,

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:26.120
you are familiar with
the API proxy bundle

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:29.210
format and all the policies
that are capable [? with-- ?]

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:30.470
that you can do with it.

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:33.680
And you know that microgateway
and Istio cannot do all

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:36.500
the policies that the
edge gateway does,

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:38.960
the gateway that you see in
the Cloud or that you see

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:40.730
[? in OPDK. ?]

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.800
So they give you a
limited set of policies.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:45.710
But our customers
wanted all the policies

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:48.380
that Cloud had to
offer available to them

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:51.320
as close to the
APIs as possible.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:53.780
The second, both
microgateway and Istio

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:55.940
use a YAML-based
declaration, which

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:57.830
means that you have
a different bundle

00:16:57.830 --> 00:17:01.520
format between Cloud and
between your hybrid offerings.

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:05.480
And really what they wanted
was a universal common method

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:09.319
in which the same bundle
format should work for Cloud,

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:11.480
should work for hybrid,
should work on-prem.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.099
And there should be no
differences between them.

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:17.430
And in response to this
evolving use cases,

00:17:17.430 --> 00:17:20.569
really what they're
asking for is the ability

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:23.750
to take the enterprise gateway
that we have in the Cloud that

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.780
is capable of all this,
doing the same proxy bundles,

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:29.520
creating all the policies
that are available,

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:33.770
including the programmability
aspect of the edge gateway,

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:37.160
and have that
available as hybrid.

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:41.210
And that's exactly what Apigee
announced at Google Next

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:46.910
on the 9th of April, where we
have now in beta Apigee hybrid.

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:49.850
So although Apigee had
two hybrid offerings

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:51.890
in terms of
microgateway and Istio,

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:55.160
this is the Apigee
enterprise gateway

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:56.900
working in the hybrid mode.

00:17:56.900 --> 00:17:59.780
Fundamentally or principally,
it works the same

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:01.880
as the other two gateways.

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:05.250
It still has a notion of a
centrally managed control

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:07.220
plane managed by Apigee.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:11.420
So Apigee would be responsible
for the UI RBAC Analytics

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:13.250
portal, and so on.

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:16.670
And customers would be
responsible for the enterprise

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:20.630
gateway that is now decoupled
from the control plane.

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:23.150
And they can deploy
the enterprise gateway

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:26.990
in the data centers and other
Cloud providers, and so on.

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:29.330
There is an important
difference here.

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:33.620
This enterprise gateway is
available to be deployed only

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:34.880
on Kubernetes.

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:37.760
Now, we will discuss as we
go through the demo what

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:40.490
Kubernetes flavors and what
are the requirements are.

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:42.110
And we'll get into
[? details. ?]

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:45.380
But this is, again, a
change of how customers

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:47.780
would consume Apigee software.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:51.380
Our [? OPDK ?] version, for
example, is available on VMs.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:54.290
A hybrid is available
on containers

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:56.550
to be run on Kubernetes.

00:18:56.550 --> 00:19:01.850
So when we talk about hybrid and
that there is a runtime plane,

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:03.950
obviously that
includes a gateway.

00:19:03.950 --> 00:19:06.200
And this is the
enterprise gateway

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:09.470
that you would deploy on
your premises or other Cloud

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:11.520
providers on Kubernetes.

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:13.820
But what does this runtime
plane really contain?

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:16.400
What is it that
you, as a customer,

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:19.010
are responsible to
manage and operate,

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:22.080
assuming that Apigee takes
care of the management plane?

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:23.840
That's where you
define the policies.

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:26.330
That's where you create the
API products, and so on.

00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:29.615
What is it that gets
shipped to customers?

00:19:29.615 --> 00:19:33.110
There are four logical
components that are shipped.

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:35.550
The first is the
message processor.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.010
This is the API Gateway.

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:38.660
For those of you
who have used OPDK,

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:40.760
you would see the
message processor.

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:43.400
That would be one of the
components that you installed.

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:46.063
And those of you who have
been using our SasS product,

00:19:46.063 --> 00:19:47.480
you wouldn't know
this, but that's

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:50.840
what we call our gateways
inside as a message processor.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:53.870
That's really the same gateway
that we've run in the Cloud

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:57.260
that you're now able
to deploy on premises.

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:00.140
What this means is that all
the proxy bundles that you've

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:02.660
built on Cloud or
on OPDK are fully

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:04.790
compatible with
the hybrid gateway.

00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:06.930
They don't need to be changed.

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:10.310
The second piece that is shipped
along with the runtime plane

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:13.080
is an agent that is
able to replicate

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:15.980
data between the
management plane

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:18.960
and where your runtime
plane is deployed.

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:20.715
So let's take an example.

00:20:20.715 --> 00:20:21.590
You have a developer.

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:24.740
Developer creates a proxy
in the management plane

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:27.900
and then deploys the proxy
to, say, the dev environment.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:30.320
Now the agent is now
listening to any changes that

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:32.090
have been happening
to the dev environment

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:35.750
and now notices that there's
now a proxy available in dev,

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:39.320
will now make that proxy
available to the gateways that

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:42.050
are running on
premises or on Cloud.

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:44.120
And that is the job
of the synchronizer.

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:46.040
It has a certain
set of credentials

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:48.500
that allows it to access
only in the environment,

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:50.870
or in a set of environments
that you give it access

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:53.420
to, and is able to
pull information

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:57.380
like target servers, proxy
bundles, resource files, things

00:20:57.380 --> 00:21:00.080
that you will create
artifacts that you create

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:02.450
in the management
plane and have them

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:04.890
made available to the gateways.

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:07.760
The third component
is Cassandra.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:10.250
Again, our OPDK
customers would know

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:13.070
that Cassandra is the
persistence mechanism we use.

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:15.110
And we continue with it.

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:18.980
Cloud customers who are
not familiar with Cassandra

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:23.180
will be pleased to know
that Cassandra is also

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:24.920
deployed on Kubernetes.

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:28.400
So we get all the benefits
of Kubernetes here.

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:31.240
We have taken time to build
the operator model for this

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:34.350
so you can manage
Kubernetes in an easier way

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:38.690
than you would if you
had deployed them on VMs.

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:41.225
And lastly, there's
a component called

00:21:41.225 --> 00:21:43.910
MART, which is an
admin server that

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:47.420
helps us manage certain
entities that are

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:49.670
required by the runtime plane.

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:54.180
The MART service is responsible
for managing entities,

00:21:54.180 --> 00:22:00.830
such as API products, API keys,
access tokens, cache, KVM.

00:22:00.830 --> 00:22:04.040
These are all entities that
are created by the runtime,

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:08.450
for the runtime, and MART
allows us to administer that.

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:10.910
So overall, these are
the four components

00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:14.540
that a customer is responsible
for and that they manage.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:18.320
And obviously the data
that is stored in Cassandra

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:20.660
never leaves the
customers' data center.

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:23.660
And it is-- or I should say
data center into your network,

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:26.180
because it could have been
in another Cloud provider.

00:22:26.180 --> 00:22:27.740
But it's data that
you never leaves

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:29.970
your approved infrastructure.

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:33.260
So what happens from a
runtime architecture?

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:35.030
How do these get deployed?

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:37.610
What is the
architecture behind it?

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:38.240
OK.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:42.655
So let's look under the hood and
see what actually gets shipped.

00:22:42.655 --> 00:22:44.780
You'll notice that there
are a few more components.

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:46.940
These are supporting components.

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:49.490
And at the end of this,
I'll talk to you a bit

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:51.560
about the philosophy
of how we have

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:54.720
built the runtime architecture.

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:58.820
The first thing is that with
Apigee hybrid, Apigee ships

00:22:58.820 --> 00:22:59.660
Istio.

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:04.250
Now, this is a version of Istio
that we have built into hybrid.

00:23:04.250 --> 00:23:06.150
You don't have to
bring your own Istio.

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:07.700
It's not a separate thing.

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:10.910
It's an Istio version that
we ship, that we manage.

00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:12.560
It's under the covers.

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:15.770
And the reason we use Istio
is because it solves a lot

00:23:15.770 --> 00:23:17.750
of problems that we need--

00:23:17.750 --> 00:23:21.440
solves the problem of doing
intelligent routing, load

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:24.960
balancing, key LS
termination, and so on.

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:27.750
So instead of us having
to reinvent that wheel,

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:30.350
we really love using
technology built

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:32.570
by our sister
organization in CSV.

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:35.880
And we are using it as
part of our architecture.

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:38.390
So what you're seeing
here, roughly each grey box

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:40.730
translates to a
pod in Kubernetes.

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:44.270
And of course, the Istio load
balance is the point of entry

00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:46.580
into the runtime architecture.

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:49.010
The Istio load balancer
is load balancing

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:51.270
the request of the message
processes, which, again,

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:53.570
is another stateless
component that

00:23:53.570 --> 00:23:56.810
is doing the proxying
before the call actually

00:23:56.810 --> 00:23:58.700
goes to your service.

00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:01.370
Supporting this infrastructure
are a few components

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:02.510
like synchronizer.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:03.350
We talked about it.

00:24:03.350 --> 00:24:06.830
It's the agent that makes proxy
bundles and other resources

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:08.240
available to the gateway.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:11.820
Cassandra is where all the
runtime data is stored.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:15.290
So the runtime data is as close
to the gateway as possible.

00:24:15.290 --> 00:24:17.570
So it eliminates any latency.

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:19.550
We also talked about
the admin server MART

00:24:19.550 --> 00:24:22.760
and that it is responsible
for managing certain artifacts

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:27.140
that the runtime relies on--
cache, KVM products, and so on.

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:29.780
And now let us talk a
bit about Analytics.

00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:31.850
How does Analytics work?

00:24:31.850 --> 00:24:34.370
So shipped along with
hybrid is an agent

00:24:34.370 --> 00:24:38.750
that collects raw analytics
data from each of the gateways

00:24:38.750 --> 00:24:41.780
and asynchronously
sends them to the Cloud.

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:47.330
So from your perspective as the
API producer or an ops person,

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:50.990
you're still going to use the
same Apigee user interface

00:24:50.990 --> 00:24:53.980
as you always did to get all
the Analytics that you're used

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:55.700
to, that you're accustomed to.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:59.030
You would not see any difference
between where the gateway is

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:00.570
actually deployed.

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:03.500
It so happens that some of
these message processors

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:05.810
are deployed outside of Apigee.

00:25:05.810 --> 00:25:08.890
But from your perspective,
you get common analytics.

00:25:08.890 --> 00:25:11.590
And the way it happens
is through this agent

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:14.440
that is able to collect
raw analytics data

00:25:14.440 --> 00:25:16.210
and send it back to the crowd.

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:20.050
But a lot of our customers have
asked if they can also get,

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:22.780
or if they can also tap
into this raw analytics

00:25:22.780 --> 00:25:26.680
information for their own data
warehousing and BI processing.

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:30.745
And this is not possible without
you having to export data out

00:25:30.745 --> 00:25:32.020
of Apigee again.

00:25:32.020 --> 00:25:36.190
The agent is capable of sending
raw analytics information

00:25:36.190 --> 00:25:38.350
to more than one destination.

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:41.110
One of those destinations
would, of course, be Apigee.

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:43.700
The second destination could
be something of your choice.

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:48.250
You could use GCS, S3, whatever
is your favorite data lake.

00:25:48.250 --> 00:25:49.540
It can be routed there.

00:25:49.540 --> 00:25:53.600
And now you have a copy of
the raw analytics, as well.

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:56.860
So the overall architecture
of what gets deployed

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:59.020
are these components.

00:25:59.020 --> 00:26:03.100
And it follows a fundamental
philosophy of open fail

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:04.700
or fail open.

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:06.030
What does that mean?

00:26:06.030 --> 00:26:09.850
You will notice that all the
critical pieces for the success

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:14.500
of your API call is all
available at the infrastructure

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:15.820
that you manage.

00:26:15.820 --> 00:26:18.370
Should you lose
connectivity to the Cloud--

00:26:18.370 --> 00:26:21.850
so that's two components, MART
and synchronizer that kind

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:23.440
of tether to the Cloud--

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:26.530
are not able to tether to the
Cloud for whatever network

00:26:26.530 --> 00:26:29.870
failure or other problems
that you may have,

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:32.830
all the information that the
gateway needs to continue

00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:35.230
processing APIs are
already available

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:37.630
and you won't
notice any failures.

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:39.160
Yes, it is true,
that you won't be

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:42.070
able to deploy a new
proxy at that point

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:44.380
or create a new cache or a KVM.

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:46.750
But any information
that already made it

00:26:46.750 --> 00:26:49.420
through before the failure
is already present.

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:51.640
And this is very critical
when running mission

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:56.080
critical applications on Apigee
that a constant tether is not

00:26:56.080 --> 00:26:58.818
necessary for your
processing of APIs.

00:26:58.818 --> 00:27:00.610
SPEAKER 1: We actually
have a few questions

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:01.580
on the architecture.

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:04.180
So why don't we take a
couple of them right now.

00:27:04.180 --> 00:27:07.490
One is, is router
and Istio the same?

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:10.360
So I presume the person
who ask this question is

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:11.590
familiar with OPDK.

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:15.050
The job off the router
that we had in OPDK

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:18.620
is now replaced by Istio's
Ingress Gateway, yes.

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:19.233
SPEAKER 1: OK.

00:27:19.233 --> 00:27:21.650
NANDAN SRIDHAR: But it does
more than what the router did.

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:23.442
So it, of course, does
what the router did.

00:27:23.442 --> 00:27:24.970
And now it does a lot more.

00:27:24.970 --> 00:27:27.610
And my following slides
will explain what

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:29.050
the additional features are.

00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:30.250
SPEAKER 1: OK.

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:33.040
Does Cassandra and runtime
architecture used--

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:35.200
is it used only for
caching, or is it

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:37.360
also temporary
storage for analytics

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:39.610
before they are
pushed to the Cloud?

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:41.860
NANDAN SRIDHAR: Casandra
is not used for analytics.

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:45.220
So Cassandra is used for
the following use cases--

00:27:45.220 --> 00:27:49.000
API products are there,
access tokens, API keys,

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:53.110
cache KVM are the entities
that are stored in Cassandra.

00:27:53.110 --> 00:27:54.790
Nothing else is stored there.

00:27:54.790 --> 00:27:56.690
And this data that
I'm talking about,

00:27:56.690 --> 00:27:59.800
which is, again, cache KBM,
access tokens, keys, products,

00:27:59.800 --> 00:28:02.380
and all that is only
stored in Cassandra.

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:04.910
There is no copy of that
maintained in the Cloud.

00:28:04.910 --> 00:28:11.427
So this data is only persisted
in the customer's Cassandra.

00:28:11.427 --> 00:28:12.010
SPEAKER 1: OK.

00:28:12.010 --> 00:28:15.430
Is there TLS between all
the runtime components

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:16.700
deployed in my data center?

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:17.690
NANDAN SRIDHAR: Yes.

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:21.130
All components have TLS or
MTLS, [? your ?] option.

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:21.730
SPEAKER 1: OK.

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:24.010
NANDAN SRIDHAR: At least
one ATLS is mandatory.

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:26.100
Two ATLS is up to you.

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:26.720
SPEAKER 1: OK.

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:27.850
Let's move on, and
we'll come back

00:28:27.850 --> 00:28:29.517
to the rest of the
questions at the end.

00:28:29.517 --> 00:28:32.110
NANDAN SRIDHAR: All right, so
what actually gets deployed,

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:35.890
and how are they
deployed on Kubernetes?

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:37.970
Like I said, the
runtime architecture

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:39.670
depends on Kubernetes.

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:43.690
It is not available on a
non-Kubernetes platform.

00:28:43.690 --> 00:28:45.220
And the reason for
that is we don't

00:28:45.220 --> 00:28:47.230
want to reinvent a
lot of the goodness

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:49.690
that Kubernetes brings
with respect to upgrades,

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:52.210
or autoscaling, and things
like that that are already

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:53.320
built into the platform.

00:28:53.320 --> 00:28:57.380
And no point in us trying
to reinvent that wheel.

00:28:57.380 --> 00:29:00.610
So most of the applications
that we deliver

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:03.280
are stateless, including
the synchronizer, which

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:04.960
is replicating data
you [INAUDIBLE],,

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:06.430
and you bring up another.

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:07.970
It's going to pull
the data again.

00:29:07.970 --> 00:29:10.880
So effectively, it is stateless.

00:29:10.880 --> 00:29:15.760
The important thing that
is stateful is Cassandra.

00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:17.320
Cassandra is a stateful set.

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:20.620
And part of the architecture
relies on at least Kubernetes

00:29:20.620 --> 00:29:22.640
1.10 or higher.

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:25.210
So if you are on a lower
version of Kubernetes,

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:28.330
you may want to think about
creating a new cluster that is

00:29:28.330 --> 00:29:30.390
at least 1.10 or higher.

00:29:30.390 --> 00:29:32.560
Now, of course, you
will get more benefits

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:34.610
when you're going to some
of the higher versions,

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:38.290
but this is the minimum
version that we are targeting.

00:29:38.290 --> 00:29:40.450
Now we've taken
some important steps

00:29:40.450 --> 00:29:43.180
here to make the operations
of these components

00:29:43.180 --> 00:29:44.870
much easier, right?

00:29:44.870 --> 00:29:48.670
So, of course, they all use
the horizontal part autoscaler

00:29:48.670 --> 00:29:50.350
of the stateless components.

00:29:50.350 --> 00:29:52.840
And when you are dealing
with a stateful component

00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:56.170
like Cassandra, we are
building automated backup.

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:58.690
And these automated
backup primarily

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:01.060
work when you're
running in other clouds

00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:04.840
like GCP, AWS, or Azure,
because they provide us

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:08.660
with some capabilities that
we can rely on to exist.

00:30:08.660 --> 00:30:10.990
And so you, as a
production operator,

00:30:10.990 --> 00:30:14.120
can configure these
automatic backup tasks.

00:30:14.120 --> 00:30:16.150
So we can take
backup of Cassandra

00:30:16.150 --> 00:30:19.060
and store it into,
say, SV or GCS,

00:30:19.060 --> 00:30:21.470
depending on where
you are running.

00:30:21.470 --> 00:30:24.430
So these are the
architectural details

00:30:24.430 --> 00:30:26.872
of how we are deploying
this on Kubernetes.

00:30:26.872 --> 00:30:29.080
Again, if there are questions,
we can come back to it

00:30:29.080 --> 00:30:30.860
at a later point in time.

00:30:30.860 --> 00:30:31.880
All right.

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:33.430
What we're going
to do now is we're

00:30:33.430 --> 00:30:35.890
going to do a demo, because
I talked to you a bit

00:30:35.890 --> 00:30:37.270
about the architecture.

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:41.650
It makes sense now for you to
see it, see the demo in action.

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:43.330
And then we'll come
back to some slides

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:45.850
and I'll walk you through
some more architectural bits.

00:30:45.850 --> 00:30:47.860
And they'll start to
make sense once you

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:49.660
see the demonstration, right?

00:30:49.660 --> 00:30:52.210
And that may also give
you the opportunity

00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:55.360
to ask other questions
based on using the demo.

00:30:55.360 --> 00:30:57.490
And those questions will
come towards the end.

00:30:57.490 --> 00:31:01.330
OK, so let's start
with the demo, OK?

00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:04.430
Let me quickly walk you
through the setup that I have.

00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:05.710
I've got two clusters here.

00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:08.530
And, of course,
I'm using GKE here.

00:31:08.530 --> 00:31:11.530
I've got one cluster,
Apigee, and that already

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:13.330
has everything configured.

00:31:13.330 --> 00:31:14.890
Everything is set up.

00:31:14.890 --> 00:31:17.680
But that's not the demo
I want to show you.

00:31:17.680 --> 00:31:20.350
The demo I want to show you
is a cluster that I've got,

00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:21.340
which is Webinar.

00:31:21.340 --> 00:31:23.830
And you'll notice here,
when I click on workloads,

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:27.240
all the things that I've created
are on the Apigee clusters.

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:30.541
The webinar cluster
is completely empty.

00:31:30.541 --> 00:31:34.330
Right, and what I want to
show you today is first,

00:31:34.330 --> 00:31:35.632
how did I set up this cluster?

00:31:35.632 --> 00:31:37.090
What are some of
the best practices

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:38.500
when you set up clusters?

00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:40.840
And then going from 0 to 60.

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:45.130
All I have is an empty cluster
and I want to get hybrid going.

00:31:45.130 --> 00:31:46.150
How quick is that?

00:31:46.150 --> 00:31:48.130
What does it mean for me?

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:51.040
So take a minute
to spend about what

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:55.503
is it that I have done to my
Apigee, to my GKE clutsers.

00:31:55.503 --> 00:31:56.920
So on this Webinar
cluster, you'll

00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:59.800
notice that I've
got two node pools.

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:01.780
I've got a node pool
for the runtime,

00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:03.820
which I've got three nodes for.

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:07.195
And I've got one separate
node pool for the data,

00:32:07.195 --> 00:32:11.170
for my stateful set,
which is Cassandra.

00:32:11.170 --> 00:32:14.050
Usually what we would recommend
is that your stateful set

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:16.370
has a high speed data.

00:32:16.370 --> 00:32:20.950
So either a local storage
class or a local with SSD

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:24.890
would be ideal from a
performance perspective.

00:32:24.890 --> 00:32:27.130
Your runtime is a stateless.

00:32:27.130 --> 00:32:29.660
It can be its own node pool.

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:35.210
And obviously, they can scale
independently of each other.

00:32:35.210 --> 00:32:36.843
Ah, zoom in.

00:32:36.843 --> 00:32:39.890
Right, is this better?

00:32:39.890 --> 00:32:42.140
Cool.

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:45.530
You also notice that I've
got something interesting

00:32:45.530 --> 00:32:48.590
going on here with
respect to leveraging

00:32:48.590 --> 00:32:51.200
Secrets and encrypting them.

00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:53.442
With [? hybrid-- ?]
and we'll go into this,

00:32:53.442 --> 00:32:54.720
into some more detail--

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:56.480
I've got to slide on this.

00:32:56.480 --> 00:33:02.000
A lot of the application
Secrets like encryption keys,

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:04.130
your [? not-bound ?]
TLS certificates,

00:33:04.130 --> 00:33:08.330
and so on, are leveraging
Kubernetes Secrets.

00:33:08.330 --> 00:33:10.250
And when you leverage
Kubernetes Secrets

00:33:10.250 --> 00:33:13.460
on platforms such
as GKE, you could

00:33:13.460 --> 00:33:18.620
use something like an HSM
or KMS as the repository

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:20.060
for where these are stored.

00:33:20.060 --> 00:33:22.580
And these are then
made available to this.

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:26.000
So anybody in industries
where they're highly

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:30.440
regulated, the ability to store
such sensitive data on an HSM

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:32.070
will be very useful.

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:34.920
So I turned this on so you
can see how that works.

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:37.520
So very, very briefly,
that's what I've got.

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:41.060
I've got a single node, or
a single region cluster.

00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:43.960
And I'm going to set
up Apigee on this--

00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:46.280
the hybrid on this.

00:33:46.280 --> 00:33:48.200
The second piece
I want to bring up

00:33:48.200 --> 00:33:54.880
is that there is
a YAML file where

00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:57.380
you specify the details
of, what is it that you

00:33:57.380 --> 00:33:59.120
want installed on this cluster?

00:33:59.120 --> 00:34:01.070
Because while you have
these four components,

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:03.990
they don't necessarily all have
to go into the same cluster.

00:34:03.990 --> 00:34:05.960
You could move
your stateful text

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:09.170
to one cluster, your stateless
components to another cluster,

00:34:09.170 --> 00:34:10.100
and so on.

00:34:10.100 --> 00:34:12.980
Given that flexibility, you need
some sort of a configuration

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:16.550
file to know where goes what.

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:19.130
So here is a YAML
file that I've got,

00:34:19.130 --> 00:34:21.440
which we call the
overrides.YAML.

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:24.469
And I've specified
in it certain details

00:34:24.469 --> 00:34:29.719
about, for example, what is
the credentials I need to pull

00:34:29.719 --> 00:34:32.030
the images, the docket images.

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:34.418
First, we signed all
our docket images

00:34:34.418 --> 00:34:35.960
and then made
available in Docker Hub

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:39.170
so you can configure your
cluster to trust images that

00:34:39.170 --> 00:34:40.730
are only signed by us.

00:34:40.730 --> 00:34:43.670
Second, you specify things
like what certificates

00:34:43.670 --> 00:34:45.710
to use for my runtime,
what certificates

00:34:45.710 --> 00:34:49.610
to use for Cassandra,
whether you want MTLS or TLS.

00:34:49.610 --> 00:34:53.544
These are all things that you
specify in this overrides file.

00:34:53.544 --> 00:34:57.680
Now, a lot of these details
I have explicitly said.

00:34:57.680 --> 00:34:59.810
Not all of them are
needed if you're doing

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:01.960
a very simple POC-type install.

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:03.680
You can skip a lot
of these details.

00:35:03.680 --> 00:35:06.620
And we assume some defaults,
which are not always

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:09.350
acceptable for a
production deployment.

00:35:09.350 --> 00:35:12.740
So I've got this
overrides.YAML that

00:35:12.740 --> 00:35:16.790
are specified for each component
what it is that I want.

00:35:16.790 --> 00:35:19.320
What happens next?

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:22.370
Now I'm going to
switch to my terminal.

00:35:22.370 --> 00:35:25.220
And I'm going to walk you
through what happens next.

00:35:25.220 --> 00:35:31.583
Apigee has shipped a utility
called Apigee Cuttle, right,

00:35:31.583 --> 00:35:33.500
kind of sticking with
the theme of Cue Cuttle,

00:35:33.500 --> 00:35:34.280
and Istio Cuttle.

00:35:34.280 --> 00:35:35.810
There's now Apigee
Cuttle that helps

00:35:35.810 --> 00:35:39.740
you manage the deployments
to the Kubernetes cluster.

00:35:39.740 --> 00:35:41.930
Now I know some
enterprises will use Helm.

00:35:41.930 --> 00:35:43.100
Some will use customize.

00:35:43.100 --> 00:35:45.500
Some will use a whole bunch
of other tools to manage it.

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:48.470
And therefore, this
is an optional tool.

00:35:48.470 --> 00:35:51.920
It is a tool that can help
you, at least initially,

00:35:51.920 --> 00:35:55.760
to understand how we generate
the Kubernetes manifests.

00:35:55.760 --> 00:36:00.050
So I use Apigee Cuttle and
pass the overrides.YAML.

00:36:00.050 --> 00:36:03.470
This allows me to create
a Kubernetes manifest.

00:36:03.470 --> 00:36:07.430
Now, if I run Apigee Cuttle
with a drive-on setting,

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:10.760
then it will only generate
the Kubernetes manifest

00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:12.470
and not actually deploy it.

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:16.340
You can, at this point,
customize it, change it,

00:36:16.340 --> 00:36:18.110
make other things
to it, and maintain

00:36:18.110 --> 00:36:20.180
your own set of
Kubernetes manifests

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:22.610
that you can deploy later.

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:25.490
And you can go into
the folder beta,

00:36:25.490 --> 00:36:28.310
you'll notice that we
use [? Go ?] templating,

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:33.470
which is kind of what other
standards like Helm also use.

00:36:33.470 --> 00:36:36.500
So it is very similar
from that perspective

00:36:36.500 --> 00:36:38.390
of what we have followed.

00:36:38.390 --> 00:36:41.570
But because not every
enterprise will have Helm--

00:36:41.570 --> 00:36:44.360
nor should we assume
that everybody has Helm--

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:48.920
this utility is handy
to get you started.

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:55.040
What I've done is I
have, in my shell script,

00:36:55.040 --> 00:36:59.450
written a couple of lines of
code that set up the namespace,

00:36:59.450 --> 00:37:04.160
create the docker, and
then run this command

00:37:04.160 --> 00:37:08.900
to apply the overrides.YAML
on my cluster.

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:10.400
So that is what I'm
going to do now.

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:12.350
I'm going to hit setup.

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:13.770
And this is going to do it.

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:15.590
And what we're going
to do at the bottom

00:37:15.590 --> 00:37:18.170
is start to watch what happens.

00:37:18.170 --> 00:37:21.920
And you'll notice below
that all these parts

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:23.150
are getting created.

00:37:23.150 --> 00:37:27.140
And we're going to give it about
a minute for this to finish,

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:28.130
right?

00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:31.220
And, in the meanwhile, while
we wait for these clusters

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:33.540
to set up, let's
take a few questions.

