00:44:27.770 --> 00:44:31.760
OK, let's quickly take a look
at some important changes

00:44:31.760 --> 00:44:35.060
that we have done from an
architecture perspective.

00:44:35.060 --> 00:44:37.220
If you look at the
workloads, you'll

00:44:37.220 --> 00:44:41.270
notice that we are shipping
a few other pieces which

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:44.920
are new components that are
shipped as part of Apigee.

00:44:44.920 --> 00:44:47.960
Let's pick the webinar cluster.

00:44:47.960 --> 00:44:53.510
Here we have a few
components called metrics,

00:44:53.510 --> 00:44:54.980
and then we have logger.

00:44:54.980 --> 00:44:55.760
Right?

00:44:55.760 --> 00:44:59.150
So with metrics, Apigee
ships Prometheus.

00:44:59.150 --> 00:45:02.910
And Prometheus is
important for two reasons.

00:45:02.910 --> 00:45:05.270
First, Prometheus
because the relay point.

00:45:05.270 --> 00:45:09.450
From there, we push
all the analytics

00:45:09.450 --> 00:45:11.550
to Stackdriver metrics.

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:16.160
Now Stackdriver metrics will
be offered as part of Apigee,

00:45:16.160 --> 00:45:17.300
or Apigee hybrid.

00:45:17.300 --> 00:45:20.000
This is not a separate thing
that you have to go get.

00:45:20.000 --> 00:45:23.630
But it will be included as
part of Apigee Monitoring.

00:45:23.630 --> 00:45:26.720
So you get basic
alerting, monitoring,

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:28.220
all those things
that you would have

00:45:28.220 --> 00:45:31.640
needed to manage your runtime
are included with this.

00:45:31.640 --> 00:45:34.370
Stackdriver automatically
gives us these capabilities,

00:45:34.370 --> 00:45:38.130
and those are included
in the platform.

00:45:38.130 --> 00:45:40.730
The second piece
is that we include

00:45:40.730 --> 00:45:44.120
the Apigee [? Logger, ?]
which automatically sends

00:45:44.120 --> 00:45:46.040
logs to Stackdriver.

00:45:46.040 --> 00:45:49.670
Again, all the logs from your
gateways, from your Cassandra

00:45:49.670 --> 00:45:51.230
and everything
else automatically

00:45:51.230 --> 00:45:57.920
goes to Stackdriver, where you
get a robust logging search

00:45:57.920 --> 00:46:01.970
mechanism that is already
shipped as part of the product.

00:46:01.970 --> 00:46:04.970
Having said that, these
may not necessarily

00:46:04.970 --> 00:46:06.980
be your Enterprise standards.

00:46:06.980 --> 00:46:10.580
And if you had an Enterprise
standard to, say, AB Dynamics,

00:46:10.580 --> 00:46:13.380
New Relic, or something
else, then, by all means,

00:46:13.380 --> 00:46:15.950
you should be able to
configure those as well.

00:46:15.950 --> 00:46:18.740
Because we use Prometheus
as our relay system,

00:46:18.740 --> 00:46:21.290
you could use
Prometheus as a point.

00:46:21.290 --> 00:46:24.230
From there, New Relic, or AB
Dynamics or something else

00:46:24.230 --> 00:46:26.210
picks it up from there.

00:46:26.210 --> 00:46:28.490
Because we use something
like [? Fluende ?]

00:46:28.490 --> 00:46:30.920
to ship data back
to Stackdriver,

00:46:30.920 --> 00:46:32.750
that could be the
point where you also

00:46:32.750 --> 00:46:36.390
add a [INAUDIBLE] ship logs
to other platforms as well.

00:46:36.390 --> 00:46:40.070
So, while Apigee will certainly
be opinionated and provide

00:46:40.070 --> 00:46:43.760
these dashboards through
a Google GCP-centric

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:46.640
view of Stackdriver
logging in metrics,

00:46:46.640 --> 00:46:51.230
you're free to configure
other platforms as well.

00:46:51.230 --> 00:46:55.635
What happens with environments
and load balancing and routing?

00:46:55.635 --> 00:46:58.010
And this is part of the reason
why we introduce something

00:46:58.010 --> 00:46:59.930
like Istio.

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:02.690
With hybrid, we want to
[? stop ?] environments

00:47:02.690 --> 00:47:05.420
as more as SDLC
platform where you have

00:47:05.420 --> 00:47:08.120
a dev, a QA, a test, and so on.

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:11.930
We have to start thinking
of environment as sandboxes.

00:47:11.930 --> 00:47:13.020
Why is that?

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:16.080
Well, we've shrunk the size
of the method processors

00:47:16.080 --> 00:47:17.360
so it can be containerized.

00:47:17.360 --> 00:47:19.725
And you can run multiple
parts in the same worker

00:47:19.725 --> 00:47:22.020
node in Kubernetes.

00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:24.770
Second, we've made
environments self-serviceable

00:47:24.770 --> 00:47:27.780
so you can create as many
environments as you want.

00:47:27.780 --> 00:47:30.930
So the environments have
now become sandboxes.

00:47:30.930 --> 00:47:32.990
So create many environments.

00:47:32.990 --> 00:47:35.120
If you're running
production, create

00:47:35.120 --> 00:47:37.790
prod-one, prod-two, prod-three,
prod-four, and so on,

00:47:37.790 --> 00:47:38.930
many environments.

00:47:38.930 --> 00:47:43.280
And each environment as
sandbox which contains

00:47:43.280 --> 00:47:45.630
proxies of similar workload.

00:47:45.630 --> 00:47:48.110
So now you don't have a
noisy neighbor situation.

00:47:48.110 --> 00:47:50.900
One proxy gets massive
amount of traffic.

00:47:50.900 --> 00:47:54.320
Other proxies are not being
autoscaled as a result.

00:47:54.320 --> 00:47:56.390
It's only that proxy that is.

00:47:56.390 --> 00:47:58.970
Now, in the previous
model, the challenge

00:47:58.970 --> 00:48:02.000
with having many environments
means that your virtual host

00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:02.990
is also many.

00:48:02.990 --> 00:48:05.600
And that's a type of user
experience you don't want

00:48:05.600 --> 00:48:10.170
to have, prod-one.API.com,
prod-two.API.com, and so on.

00:48:10.170 --> 00:48:13.100
But with the load balancing
that we're doing with Istio,

00:48:13.100 --> 00:48:15.800
we are now able to make
it intelligent enough

00:48:15.800 --> 00:48:17.630
where the load balancer
is able to send

00:48:17.630 --> 00:48:20.930
that request to the
appropriate environment, right?

00:48:20.930 --> 00:48:23.570
So, although you have
many environments,

00:48:23.570 --> 00:48:26.810
they will have the same domain
name, or the same virtual host,

00:48:26.810 --> 00:48:29.150
to which they access their APIs.

00:48:29.150 --> 00:48:30.830
So you get your isolation.

00:48:30.830 --> 00:48:32.630
You get the scaling
that you need,

00:48:32.630 --> 00:48:36.290
while not having to reveal some
of these infrastructure details

00:48:36.290 --> 00:48:37.940
to your consumer.

00:48:37.940 --> 00:48:40.040
A question I get
asked a lot is, hey,

00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:41.630
I'm not using Istio
as my ingress.

00:48:41.630 --> 00:48:42.740
I'm using something else.

00:48:42.740 --> 00:48:44.520
What happens?

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:46.490
Well, first off, to
be pedantic, it still

00:48:46.490 --> 00:48:47.990
doesn't use the
ingress notation,

00:48:47.990 --> 00:48:49.740
it uses a gateway notation.

00:48:49.740 --> 00:48:53.160
So it's not really an ingress
from that perspective.

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:56.450
But feel free to add something
else on top of the Istio

00:48:56.450 --> 00:48:59.760
ingress, like an F5 load
balancer or something else.

00:48:59.760 --> 00:49:03.600
But the Istio ingress is a
part of the Apigee install.

00:49:03.600 --> 00:49:04.370
We control it.

00:49:04.370 --> 00:49:06.500
And we need that
to be there to be

00:49:06.500 --> 00:49:08.990
able to do some of this
intelligence self-balancing

00:49:08.990 --> 00:49:10.130
that we do.

00:49:10.130 --> 00:49:15.050
Two, the TLS search and keys
that are used for the ingress

00:49:15.050 --> 00:49:17.180
are sorted in
Kubernetes Secrets.

00:49:17.180 --> 00:49:19.340
So that's not
information that even

00:49:19.340 --> 00:49:20.985
comes to the control plane.

00:49:20.985 --> 00:49:23.750
Now, we talked a bit
about Secrets management.

00:49:23.750 --> 00:49:27.680
Secrets are used in a
number of instances.

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:30.050
We use Secrets to
store encryption keys

00:49:30.050 --> 00:49:31.730
for your encrypted KVM.

00:49:31.730 --> 00:49:35.090
We also use encryption
keys to encrypt your cache.

00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:38.510
All of these secrets are now
in the Kubernetes Secret.

00:49:38.510 --> 00:49:42.320
And in the example
I showed with GKE,

00:49:42.320 --> 00:49:45.800
I used an HSM to store
my encryption key.

00:49:45.800 --> 00:49:48.020
Also, your TLS secrets
are stored there.

00:49:48.020 --> 00:49:50.330
And if you're using
Kubernetes 1.13,

00:49:50.330 --> 00:49:54.260
all the information in
[? SCD ?] is also encrypted.

00:49:54.260 --> 00:49:56.240
How would multi-region
expansion work?

00:49:56.240 --> 00:49:59.828
Well, most of these
components are stateless.

00:49:59.828 --> 00:50:01.370
The only stateful
thing is Cassandra.

00:50:01.370 --> 00:50:04.490
And you would expand this by
creating additional clusters

00:50:04.490 --> 00:50:09.210
and creating a ring that
expands multiple regions.

00:50:09.210 --> 00:50:12.230
All right, so now we've
got three options, right?

00:50:12.230 --> 00:50:13.280
We've got microgateway.

00:50:13.280 --> 00:50:14.510
We've got the Istio Adapter.

00:50:14.510 --> 00:50:15.800
And we've got hybrid.

00:50:15.800 --> 00:50:17.820
What option is right for you?

00:50:17.820 --> 00:50:20.840
So, first off, let's understand
what features are there

00:50:20.840 --> 00:50:24.350
in the Apigee platform and
what's available in hybrid.

00:50:24.350 --> 00:50:28.610
It is Apigee's goal to
make Apigee hybrid feature

00:50:28.610 --> 00:50:30.410
equivalent to Cloud.

00:50:30.410 --> 00:50:34.160
It shouldn't matter who
manages your runtime, Apigee

00:50:34.160 --> 00:50:35.420
or our customers.

00:50:35.420 --> 00:50:37.610
All the features that
you get in the Cloud,

00:50:37.610 --> 00:50:40.160
you should get with hybrid.

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:44.120
Some of these examples, like
bot detection or [? holstered ?]

00:50:44.120 --> 00:50:45.980
targets, are currently
not available

00:50:45.980 --> 00:50:49.370
in our on-premises version,
only available in Cloud.

00:50:49.370 --> 00:50:51.440
But with hybrid, all
of these features

00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:55.050
will eventually be available
with hybrid as well.

00:50:55.050 --> 00:50:57.530
So with that, here's
a quick summary.

00:50:57.530 --> 00:50:59.840
And I'm not going to
draw too much into this.

00:50:59.840 --> 00:51:02.400
We'll make available these
slides through the webinar.

00:51:02.400 --> 00:51:05.592
So you will have access
to this information.

00:51:05.592 --> 00:51:07.550
But I'm going to quickly
go through, because we

00:51:07.550 --> 00:51:09.350
are running out of time here.

00:51:09.350 --> 00:51:12.270
Let me very quickly give you
some guidance on what you pick

00:51:12.270 --> 00:51:14.870
[? when. ?] The first
question you need to ask

00:51:14.870 --> 00:51:16.880
is, do you want SaaS or hybrid?

00:51:16.880 --> 00:51:19.610
Which is, do you really want
to manage anything at all,

00:51:19.610 --> 00:51:23.420
or do you want to let Apigee
manage all the infrastructure?

00:51:23.420 --> 00:51:27.210
If hybrid is the answer, then
the next question becomes,

00:51:27.210 --> 00:51:29.990
well, what are you trying
to manage the APIs for?

00:51:29.990 --> 00:51:32.010
Are they for
microservices, or are they

00:51:32.010 --> 00:51:34.370
for a mix of old and new?

00:51:34.370 --> 00:51:36.260
So if it is just
microservices, then you

00:51:36.260 --> 00:51:39.920
could use either microgateway
or Istio, if you're using Istio.

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:42.710
And then that becomes
your choice of gateway.

00:51:42.710 --> 00:51:45.320
If your policies are going
to be more complicated

00:51:45.320 --> 00:51:47.900
than that, where you want
to have all the advanced

00:51:47.900 --> 00:51:50.500
features of the
Apigee Edge platform,

00:51:50.500 --> 00:51:54.470
then Apigee hybrid would be
the right answer for you.

00:51:54.470 --> 00:51:56.170
So this is a very,
very high level view.

00:51:56.170 --> 00:51:59.140
I'm positive that,
when you actually

00:51:59.140 --> 00:52:01.540
get into individual
customers scenarios,

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:04.180
there'll be more
questions than this.

00:52:04.180 --> 00:52:06.910
I'm going to take 30
seconds to talk about Anthos

00:52:06.910 --> 00:52:09.010
and how we run with Anthos.

00:52:09.010 --> 00:52:12.880
Anthos ships with GKE,
eventually with CSM and Cloud

00:52:12.880 --> 00:52:13.480
Run.

00:52:13.480 --> 00:52:17.380
Apigee would be what's
on top of GKE on-prem.

00:52:17.380 --> 00:52:20.410
And eventually, we'd be looking
for integration with CSM

00:52:20.410 --> 00:52:22.800
